</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\{s_{1},...s_{N}\}}Z=\max\left(Y,X\right)=\max\left(\sum_{i=1}^{M}y_{i},\ \sum_{j=1}^{N}x_{j}^{s_{j}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\{s_{1},...s_{N}\}}Z=\max\left(Y,X\right)=\max\left(\sum_{i=1}^{M}y_{i},\ \sum_{j=1}^{N}x_{j}^{s_{j}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we now introduce a unified cost model and analyze its behavior across different bottleneck scenarios in computation-communication overlap.
For an overlap, let it contain <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> computation operators and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> communication operators.
We denote the execution time of the i-th computation operator as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the execution time of the j-th communication operator under configuration <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{s_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{s_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
With computations and communications serialized on separate CUDA streams, the total computation time is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\sum_{i=1}^{M}y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\sum_{i=1}^{M}y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the total communication time is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\sum_{j=1}^{N}x_{j}^{s_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\sum_{j=1}^{N}x_{j}^{s_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the overall makespan is given by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=max(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=max(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As shown in Equation (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1. Problem Definition ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>),
the optimization objective is to minimize the overall makespan <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> by selecting optimal configurations <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all communication operators.
We now analyze the two bottleneck scenarios separately.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.SS1.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_centering ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\{s_{1},...s_{N}\}}Z=\min_{\{s_{1}...s_{N}\}}\sum_{j=1}^{N}x_{j}^{s_{j}}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\min_{s_{j}}x_{j}^{s_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\{s_{1},...s_{N}\}}Z=\min_{\{s_{1}...s_{N}\}}\sum_{j=1}^{N}x_{j}^{s_{j}}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\min_{s_{j}}x_{j}^{s_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Communication Bottleneck.</span>
First, in the communication-bound case described by Equation <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1. Problem Definition ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the overlap makespan simplifies to the cumulative time of all communications.
The key challenges are: (1) modeling how configuration <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> affects communication time <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{s_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{s_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (2) capturing the impact of computational contention on communication.
AutoCCL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> addresses this by employing a subspace coordinate descent method for search, and leverages online sampling with real-time feedback to avoid explicit modeling of the contention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\{s_{1}...s_{N}\}}Z=\sum_{i=1}^{M}y_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\{s_{1}...s_{N}\}}Z=\sum_{i=1}^{M}y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computation Bottleneck.</span>
Symmetrically, Equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1. Problem Definition ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> describes the computation-bound scenario, where the overlap makespan is determined by the sum of computation operator times.
Two key challenges arise in this case: (1) Communication parameters influence computational performance by governing how communications utilize shared resources (further discussed in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>);
(2) Due to the serialized execution of communications, tuning an earlier communication changes the start time and overlap window of subsequent operators, creating a cascade effect on the computation. This requires considering the entire configuration set <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{s_{1},s_{2},\dots,s_{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{s_{1},s_{2},\dots,s_{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> jointly. With the configuration space per communication exceeding <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> options <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the joint search space becomes <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(r^{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(r^{N})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> communication operators, which is computationally intractable for practical values of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now analyze how to tune communication parameters in the computation-bound overlap scenario.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Contention Modeling</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:189.8pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20656v1/change_nc_c.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:262/233;" width="262" height="233"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Various <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NC</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:138.0pt;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20656v1/change_nc.svg" id="S3.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/106;" width="191" height="106"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Various <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NC</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20656v1/change_c.svg" id="S3.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/111;" width="191" height="111"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.fig2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.fig2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Various <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Duration of FFN when AllReduce(32MB) with various NC and C (NT=128) on 8 <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> A40pcie</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on AutoCCL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we adopt a divide-and-conquer strategy based on implementation-related parameters (Algorithm, Protocol, Transport) before analyzing the effect of resource-related parameters.
Our key finding is that while different communication configurations can achieve similar communication performance, they exhibit significant variation in how much they slow down computation.
As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, when using NC=16 versus NC=32, communication performance remains nearly identical, but computation time differs by 30.2%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted separate tests on NC, NT, and C, and found that NC and C have a significant impact on computation time, while the effect of NT is negligible.
Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the overall impact of different NC and C configurations on computation time, measured by concurrently running a 32 MB AllReduce and an FFN operator on 8 NVIDIA A40 GPUs with PCIe interconnect.
The results indicate that increasing either NC or C progressively prolongs computation time.
To isolate individual factors, Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows how communication and computation times vary as NC increases while <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=16KB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=16KB</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the effect of increasing C when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NC=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NC=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
The figures confirm that while larger NC and C values initially reduce communication time (with diminishing returns or even slight increases at larger values), they also significantly slow down computation.
These findings were reproduced consistently on another cluster.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:158.7pt;"><img src="2602.20656v1/contention_0.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:219/251;" width="219" height="251" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F4.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Computation Only</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;"><img src="2602.20656v1/contention_1.png" id="S3.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:229/262;" width="229" height="262" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F4.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Communication Contention</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SM and global resource contention.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost Model</span>
We characterize communication-computation contention along two dimensions: SM competition and global resource competition.
As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, SM competition arises from shared processor occupancy, while global resource contention stems from bandwidth saturation.
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SM Competition.</span>
Communication operators occupy a portion of SMs, reducing the resources available for computation and increasing the number of computation waves.
The assignment of threadblocks, whether for computation or communication, to an SM is constrained by the availability of threads, shared memory, and registers.
Due to this multi-constraint bottleneck, NT has a negligible impact on SM competition.
NC is the dominant factor governing SM occupancy.
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Global Resource Competition.</span> Communication also contends for shared global resources such as memory bandwidth and cache.
Each communication operator launches NC threadblocks that read/write data at a granularity of C.
Memory transactions from different threads within a threadblock are coalesced, making NT’s impact on global contention relatively minor.
In contrast, larger values of NC and C generate more intensive contention due to increased concurrent data movement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{j=l}^{k}f_{ij}\times g_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{j=l}^{k}f_{ij}\times g_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lceil\frac{\mu_{i}}{(\lambda-NC_{j})\times TB_{i}}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lceil\frac{\mu_{i}}{(\lambda-NC_{j})\times TB_{i}}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\theta_{ij}+\frac{(\lambda-NC_{j})\times TB_{i}\times D_{i}}{\overline{B}-V(NC_{j},C_{j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\theta_{ij}+\frac{(\lambda-NC_{j})\times TB_{i}\times D_{i}}{\overline{B}-V(NC_{j},C_{j})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Based on these observations, we build a cost model of the computation <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> overlapped with communication <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l-k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) decomposes computation time <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into wave count <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and per-wave latency <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> that is overlapped with the j-th communication.
Eq.(<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) shows the number of computation waves <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by dividing the total required thread blocks <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by the number of available threadblocks per SM after allocating <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NC_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NC_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> SMs to communication.
Meanwhile, Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) shows <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of computation time <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and data transfer time, where the latter depends on the data volume <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the effective bandwidth reduced by global resource contention <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(NC_{j},C_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(NC_{j},C_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notation definitions are summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1. Problem Definition ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
A higher NC occupies more SMs, reducing the resources available for computation and consequently increasing the number of waves <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, while also imposing additional demand on bandwidth.
A larger C, in turn, amplifies contention for global bandwidth, thereby extending the per<span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>wave execution time <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Contention Modeling ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> empirically validates our model: increasing NC/C beyond optimal points (e.g., NC&gt;8) drastically prolongs computation time despite marginal communication gains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Priority Metric</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When multiple communication and computation tasks execute concurrently, the central challenge lies in efficiently identifying near-optimal configurations from a combinatorial space that scales as <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1,000,000^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>1,000,000</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1,000,000^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Brute-force searching this space is infeasible, necessitating the use of heuristic methods to find high-quality solutions within practical time constraints.
A naive strategy is to optimize communications sequentially. However, this approach fails to account for critical interdependencies: tuning one communication alters its execution timeline, which cascades to affect both the communication and computation phases of subsequent tasks. These temporal and resource-contentions introduce nonlinear interactions that render sequential optimization highly suboptimal.
To illustrate this, an experiment was conducted on A40 GPUs with 2 AllReduce and 7 MatMul executed concurrently.
We adjust <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NC</annotation></semantics></math> of one communication at a time to analyze how it impacts overall performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.3. Priority Metric ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, increasing NC from 1 to 16 affects the two communications differently.
The slope of each arrow indicates the rate of change in computation and communication times.
Optimizing Communication B achieves a larger reduction in communication time with only a slight increase in computation time, resulting in a shorter total execution time.
In contrast, tuning Communication A provides smaller benefits.
These results reveal that optimizing different communications yields distinct trade-offs between communication gains and computational overhead.
The overall performance improvement thus depends on selectively prioritizing the communications that provide higher benefits.
This motivates the introduction of the metric <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, which quantifies such trade<span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>offs across concurrent communications.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20656v1/tune_diff_comm_a.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/107;" width="229" height="107"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F5.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">tuning AllReduce A only</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F5.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20656v1/tune_diff_comm_b.svg" id="S3.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/107;" width="229" height="107"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F5.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">tuning AllReduce B only</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cost differences when tuning different communications in multiple communication scenarios</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric H.</span>
We define a priority metric <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for the j-th communication as Equation (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.3. Priority Metric ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the computation times before and after tuning, while <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{s_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{s_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{s^{\prime}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{s^{\prime}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the corresponding communication times for the j-th communication.
Where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the computation time before and after optimization, respectively, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{s_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{s_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{s^{\prime}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{s^{\prime}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the communication time corresponding to the two configurations <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for the j-th communication.</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}=\frac{Y^{\prime}-Y}{x_{j}^{s_{j}}-x_{j}^{s^{\prime}_{j}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}=\frac{Y^{\prime}-Y}{x_{j}^{s_{j}}-x_{j}^{s^{\prime}_{j}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, a smaller numerator indicates a lower computational overhead, and a larger denominator implies greater communication improvement.
The metric <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> measures the tuning efficiency, expressed as the communication improvement achieved per unit of added computation cost. A negative denominator implies that the communication is already optimal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Noted: in multi-computation scenarios, tuning one communication affects multiple overlapped computations, making explicit modeling of each interference infeasible. Since <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> inherently reflects the overall impact of the tuning on all computations, the analysis can be simplified by considering only the aggregate computation time <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, significantly reducing complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Search Method</h3>

<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Cost-Effectiveness Algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">


<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> Communication config set <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{s_{1},s_{2},...,s_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{s_{1},s_{2},...,s_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span> fine-tuned communication config <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}\leftarrow\{\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}\leftarrow\{\}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            Initialize all <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.01
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s.done=\text{False}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">False</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s.done=\text{False}</annotation></semantics></math> </em> <span id="algorithm1.4.5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}\leftarrow\arg\min\limits_{s_{i}\in M,s_{i}.done=\text{False}}H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>False</mtext></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}\leftarrow\arg\min\limits_{s_{i}\in M,s_{i}.done=\text{False}}H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}\leftarrow\text{ResourceEfficientTuning}(s_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>ResourceEfficientTuning</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}\leftarrow\text{ResourceEfficientTuning}(s_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.8.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}.done" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}.done</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}.\text{append}(s_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>append</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}.\text{append}(s_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{j}\leftarrow\frac{Y^{\prime}-Y}{x_{j}^{s_{j}}-x_{j}^{s^{\prime}_{j}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{j}\leftarrow\frac{Y^{\prime}-Y}{x_{j}^{s_{j}}-x_{j}^{s^{\prime}_{j}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            end if
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
            end while
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.14.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></em>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.15" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In an overlap, the optimal communication configuration satisfies one of the following conditions: (1) All communications use minimal resources, and their total time cost is less than the computation time. (2) Communications are fully optimized but still take longer than computation, and further increasing communication resources prolongs both communication and computation time. (3) Increasing communication resources reduces communication time but increases computation time until the two are equal—beyond this point, further resource allocation for communication continues to speed up communication while slowing computation even more, degrading overall performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm2" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span>Resource-Efficient Tuning Algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm2.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">


<span id="algorithm2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> Communication config <math id="algorithm2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not initialized</em> <span id="algorithm2.4.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}.NC,s_{j}^{\prime}.NT,s_{j}^{\prime}.C\leftarrow NC_{min},NT_{min},C_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}.NC,s_{j}^{\prime}.NT,s_{j}^{\prime}.C\leftarrow NC_{min},NT_{min},C_{min}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            end if
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{s_{j}^{\prime}},Y^{\prime},X^{\prime}\leftarrow\text{ProfileTime}(s_{j}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>ProfileTime</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{s_{j}^{\prime}},Y^{\prime},X^{\prime}\leftarrow\text{ProfileTime}(s_{j}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.6.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{s_{j}^{\prime}}-x_{j}^{s_{j}}&gt;0\text{ or }X^{\prime}&lt;Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> or </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{s_{j}^{\prime}}-x_{j}^{s_{j}}&gt;0\text{ or }X^{\prime}&lt;Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm2.4.6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}.done\leftarrow\text{True}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mtext>True</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}.done\leftarrow\text{True}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr\leftarrow\frac{x_{j}^{s_{j}^{\prime}}-x_{j}^{s_{j}}}{x_{j}^{s_{j}^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr\leftarrow\frac{x_{j}^{s_{j}^{\prime}}-x_{j}^{s_{j}}}{x_{j}^{s_{j}^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}.NC\leftarrow s_{j}^{\prime}.NC+lr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}.NC\leftarrow s_{j}^{\prime}.NC+lr</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}.NT\leftarrow s_{j}^{\prime}.NT+lr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}.NT\leftarrow s_{j}^{\prime}.NT+lr</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}.C\leftarrow s_{j}^{\prime}.C+lr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}.C\leftarrow s_{j}^{\prime}.C+lr</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span>
            end if
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algorithm2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To efficiently identify the optimal configuration that satisfies the boundary conditions, we designed Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.4. Search Method ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In 3.4. Search Method ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.4. Search Method ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span> iteratively optimizes communications within an overlap group.
In each iteration, it selects the communication with the smallest <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> from the unfinished set (line 4), then invokes Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In 3.4. Search Method ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to determine the next configuration for the selected communication (line 5).
If the communication is marked as completed, its configuration is added to the output set (line 7).
Otherwise, the algorithm updates the corresponding <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> based on the latest measurements (line 9).
The process repeats until all communications have been tuned.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In 3.4. Search Method ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></span> generates the next configuration for a given communication.
If the communication has not been initialized, NC, NT, and C are set to their minimum values (lines 1-3).
Otherwise, the algorithm checks whether the current configuration satisfies either of the following termination conditions (line 5): (1) the communication itself has reached its optimal performance, or (2) the communication time is less than the computation time.
If neither condition is met, the algorithm increases the values of NC, NT, and C by a learning rate <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr</annotation></semantics></math> computed from the relative improvement in communication time (lines 8–11).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20656v1/workflow.svg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:462/213;" width="462" height="213"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Workflow of tuning multi-comms in one overlap</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We integrate the proposed algorithms into AutoCCL’s divide-and-conquer framework to tune resource-related parameters.
The overall workflow is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.4. Search Method ‣ 3. Design ‣ Lagom: Unleashing the Power of Communication and Computation Overlapping for Distributed LLM Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, using an overlap with 3 communication as an example.
The algorithm begins by selecting the communication with the smallest <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> among those not yet tuned (<span id="S3.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="comm_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">comm_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in step a).
It then increases the NC, NT, and C for this communication appropriately.
The new configuration <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is added to <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="comm_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">comm_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s configuration list (step d) and broadcast to all ranks for execution (step c).
After measuring the execution time (step e), the <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="comm_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">comm_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated (step f).
This process repeats iteratively until all communications have been tuned.
This approach identifies minimal resource-related parameter that balance communication and computation performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Evaluation</h2>

<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
